<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!-- Doc-COMPLETENESS suppression for the test tree. The root Directory.Build.props carries this
         NoWarn for src/, but this test-tree props does NOT import the root, so without it every test
         project that turns on GenerateDocumentationFile must remember to add CS1591 itself (some did,
         some didn't → AI.Test surfaced 58 CS1591 under -warnaserror). Mirrors the central src policy:
         missing member/param/typeparam docs on test types are not filled wholesale. Doc-QUALITY
         warnings (broken/ambiguous crefs, malformed XML) are NOT here — those are real bugs, fixed. -->
    <!-- xUnit1051 (pass TestContext.Current.CancellationToken to async calls) is an established
         team decision to suppress in the test tree — 14 test csprojs already carried
         `<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);xUnit1051</NoWarn>` individually; centralising it here removes the
         per-project duplication and applies it uniformly (the remaining projects only ever lacked
         it by omission). Tests use explicit `TestContext.Current.CancellationToken` + `.Timeout(...)`
         where cancellation matters. NOT a code-quality suppression: xUnit1031 (blocking task ops →
         deadlock risk) is deliberately NOT here and is fixed at the source instead. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573;CS1712;xUnit1051</NoWarn>
    <!-- NuGet transitive-dependency ADVISORY warnings (NU1901-NU1904) must not fail the test build
         under -warnaserror. The ROOT Directory.Build.props already carries exactly this policy for src/,
         but this test-tree props does NOT import the root (see the doc-completeness note above), so a
         newly-published advisory on a transitive package (e.g. AngleSharp GHSA-pgww-w46g-26qg → NU1902)
         turned green test builds RED for every test project at once while src/ stayed a warning. Mirror
         the central src policy here so the test tree tracks it uniformly. Vulnerability handling
         (bump/replace) is a deliberate dependency decision, not a per-build -warnaserror gate. -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;$(WarningsNotAsErrors)</WarningsNotAsErrors>
    <!-- Required so the global <Using> items below (incl. MeshWeaver.Reactive.Assertions)
         are actually emitted. With ImplicitUsings disabled, <Using> is silently ignored,
         and Should()/Seconds() fail to resolve across every test file. -->
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <!-- Always emit a TRX so every `dotnet test` invocation leaves a parseable
         result file in TestResults/ — required for hang diagnosis (the only
         place the per-test StdOut + outcome is recorded once the host crashes
         or the runner is killed). Without this, local runs leave nothing
         behind to inspect. CI passes `-l:trx` explicitly; this matches that
         behavior for local. Path = `<project>/TestResults/_<machine>_<utc>.trx`. -->
    <VSTestLogger>trx</VSTestLogger>
    <!-- xUnit v3 → trx StdOut: anything written via ITestOutputHelper.WriteLine
         (which the per-test ILogger redirects to at Information+ via xunit.runner.json
         + appsettings.json's MeshWeaver=Information) flows into the trx's
         <StdOut> element on the failing test result. No extra config needed
         beyond keeping diagnosticMessages false (default) and writing logs
         through the standard pipeline. -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit.ITestOutputHelper" Alias="ITestOutputHelper" />
    <!-- In-house assertion library (replaced FluentAssertions). One global using
         covers Should()/asserts + the Seconds()/Milliseconds()/… time helpers, so
         test files need no per-file assertion import. -->
    <Using Include="MeshWeaver.Reactive.Assertions" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Global project reference: every test project gets the assertion library. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\src\MeshWeaver.Reactive.Assertions\MeshWeaver.Reactive.Assertions.csproj" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3.runner.console">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    </ItemGroup>
  <!-- xunit.runner.json — the shared single-threaded default, UNLESS a project ships its own
       next to its .csproj.

       A per-project file is how a project opts INTO intra-project parallelism
       (parallelizeTestCollections + maxParallelThreads > 1) once it has been measured
       green that way. It is deliberately opt-in per project rather than a global flip:
       parallel safety is a property of the tests, not of the runner, and the suite has
       classes that share a Testcontainers instance ([Collection("PostgreSql")]) or assert
       on process-global state.

       The two includes are mutually exclusive on purpose. Two Content items both landing
       `xunit.runner.json` in the same output directory is a duplicate-copy conflict whose winner
       MSBuild picks without guaranteeing which — i.e. a project could silently get the shared
       default back and quietly lose its parallelism, or worse, get parallelism it never opted into.

       🚨 Both are `Content`, and the local one must NOT be written as `None Update`. This file is
       imported by Microsoft.Common.props, which runs BEFORE the SDK's default item globs are
       defined — so an `Update` here matches nothing and silently no-ops. The failure is not a
       missing optimisation: with no xunit.runner.json in the output at all, xUnit falls back to
       ITS OWN defaults, where parallelizeTestCollections is true and the thread count is the core
       count. A silent no-op therefore hands the project unbounded parallelism nobody opted into.
       `Content Include` works because the SDK's default None glob excludes @(Content), so the file
       is not double-copied. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="!Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/xunit.runner.json')">
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)\xunit.runner.json">
      <Link>%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/xunit.runner.json')">
    <Content Include="xunit.runner.json">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- appsettings.json — EXACTLY the same two-mutually-exclusive-branches shape as
       xunit.runner.json above, and for the same reason.

       🚨 This used to be a single UNCONDITIONAL include of the shared file, linked to
       `appsettings.json` in the output. Eleven test projects also ship their own
       appsettings.json next to their .csproj, nine of which declared their own
       `<Content Include="appsettings.json">`. Two Content items landing the same file name in
       the same output directory is a duplicate-copy conflict whose winner MSBuild picks without
       guaranteeing which — so those projects' logging configuration was decided by build order.

       The cost was not hypothetical. test/MeshWeaver.Query.Test/appsettings.json raises
       MeshWeaver.Graph / Hosting.Persistence / MeshNodeStreamCache to Debug specifically to
       diagnose the ActivityTrackingHubTest.TrackActivity_InitiatedFromConnectionHub_LandsNode
       flake (#993). Locally the project-local file wins and the channels are live; on CI run
       31301311379 the failing test's captured Output was EMPTY for its full 15 s budget and the
       whole run contained zero lines from any of those channels. The diagnostic added for that
       flake had therefore never produced a single byte of evidence on the only platform where
       the flake occurs.

       Owning both branches here (rather than leaving the local copy to each .csproj) is what
       makes them mutually exclusive; the per-project `<Content Include="appsettings.json">`
       declarations were removed in the same change, and VerifyTestConfigCopied in
       Directory.Build.targets fails the build if neither branch lands a file. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="!Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/appsettings.json')">
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)\appsettings.json">
      <Link>%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/appsettings.json')">
    <Content Include="appsettings.json">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>